            <ArticleTitle>Transdermal scopolamine patch in addition to ondansetron for postoperative nausea and vomiting prophylaxis in patients undergoing ambulatory cosmetic surgery.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>249-52</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.jclinane.2008.08.017</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="STUDY OBJECTIVE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">To determine the efficacy of transdermal scopolamine in addition to ondansetron in decreasing the incidence of postoperative nausea and vomiting (PONV).</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="DESIGN" NlmCategory="METHODS">Randomized controlled trial.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="SETTING" NlmCategory="METHODS">Academic hospital.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="PATIENTS" NlmCategory="METHODS">126 ASA physical status I and II patients undergoing outpatient plastic surgery with three or more risk factors for PONV.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="INTERVENTIONS" NlmCategory="METHODS">Patients were randomly assigned to one of two groups to receive (Group 1) a transdermal scopolamine (TDS) patch or (Group 2), a placebo patch two hours before surgery.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="MEASUREMENTS" NlmCategory="METHODS">Occurrence of vomiting, severity of nausea using a visual analog scale (VAS), rescue medication, pain intensity and pain medications, and side effects were recorded every hour until discharge from hospital, then every 4 hours thereafter for a total of 24 hours.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="MAIN RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">A statistically significant reduction in postoperative nausea between 8 and 24 hours in patients receiving TDS was noted.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSIONS" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Transdermal scopolamine in addition to ondansetron benefits patients at high risk for PONV undergoing outpatient plastic surgery for up to 20 hours after surgery.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
